ed thousand movie database and each  person will rate different movies so  maybe I ring I rate these tens someone  else rates another ten and so on and so  forth so the vast majority of this  matrix is actually missing and the  Netflix prize is essentially designed to  fill in all of the missing values of  this matrix to pinpoint which movies you  haven't seen that you would like to see  okay that you would enjoy seeing and the  entire idea of what makes this work is  the fact that this matrix has dominant  correlations among the columns and among  the rows I'm going to walk you through  that very briefly so even though we all  like to think of ourselves as unique  with our own unique preferences and and  tastes it turns out that you know if you  if you took the SVD if if all of the  values were filled in and you took the  SVD of this matrix there would still be  relatively few dominant patterns in  terms of eigen tastes of people's movie  preferences maybe there would be ten  maybe there would be a hundred but there  certainly wouldn't be a hundred million  important unique profiles okay  maybe you have people that like action  movies and Marvel superhero movies maybe  you have people that like you know  romance and comedies maybe some people  like all of them but those might be kind  of your principal components similarly  movies themselves have a lot of dominant  correlations in terms of what people  like about them you have your action  movies your romances your comedies and  so on and so forth and so for example if  person one likes The Terminator and  alien and I liked Terminator probably I  will also like alien or at least that  that's the kind of idea that would be  going on here is that you could use  correlations among the people among the  columns and correlations among the  rose to discover what would be the  hidden or missing values that have not  been filled in here okay now you can't  use the singular value decomposition  off-the-shelf to fill in all 